#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539294 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_D e^{2 x + 6 y} \\left(x - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y):1\\le x\\le 3,\\quad 2\\le x + 3 y\\le 3\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 3,\\quad 2\\le x + 3 y\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x + 3 y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):1\\le u\\le 3,\\,2\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\\\1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- \\frac{u}{3} + \\frac{v}{3}$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D e^{2 x + 6 y} \\left(x - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_\\Delta e^{2 v} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(e^{2 v} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} [\\int_{2}^{3} e^{2 v} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} \\left(u - 1\\right)[\\int_{2}^{3} e^{2 v}\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\left[\\frac{e^{2 v}}{2}\\right]_{2}^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\left(\\left(\\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)-(\\left(\\frac{e^{4}}{2}\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)\\int_{1}^{3} u - 1\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)\\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{2} - u\\right]_{1}^{3} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)\\left(\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)+\\left(-3\\right)-(\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\left(-1\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right) \\\\ & =- \\frac{e^{4}}{3} + \\frac{e^{6}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539297 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_D e^{2 x + 6 y} \\left(x - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y):2\\le x\\le 3,\\quad 2\\le x + 3 y\\le 3\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n2\\le x\\le 3,\\quad 2\\le x + 3 y\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x + 3 y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):2\\le u\\le 3,\\,2\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\\\1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- \\frac{u}{3} + \\frac{v}{3}$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D e^{2 x + 6 y} \\left(x - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_\\Delta e^{2 v} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(e^{2 v} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{2}^{3} [\\int_{2}^{3} e^{2 v} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{2}^{3} \\left(u - 1\\right)[\\int_{2}^{3} e^{2 v}\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{2}^{3} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\left[\\frac{e^{2 v}}{2}\\right]_{2}^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{2}^{3} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\left(\\left(\\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)-(\\left(\\frac{e^{4}}{2}\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)\\int_{2}^{3} u - 1\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)\\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{2} - u\\right]_{2}^{3} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)\\left(\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)+\\left(-3\\right)-(\\left(2\\right)+\\left(-2\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right) \\\\ & =- \\frac{e^{4}}{4} + \\frac{e^{6}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539305 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_D x + y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 7,\\quad 0\\le x + y\\le 3\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 7,\\quad 0\\le x + y\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x + y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 7,\\,0\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\\\1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=1=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- u + v$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D x + y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_\\Delta v\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(v\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{3} [\\int_{0}^{7} v\\,\\mathrm{d}u]\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{3} v[\\int_{0}^{7} 1\\,\\mathrm{d}u]\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{3} v\\left[u\\right]_{0}^{7}\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{3} v\\left(\\left(7\\right)-(\\left(0\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(7\\right)\\int_{0}^{3} v\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(7\\right)\\left[\\frac{v^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{3} \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(7\\right)\\left(\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)-(\\left(0\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(7\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right) \\\\ & =\\frac{63}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539318 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_D x + y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 6,\\quad 0\\le x + y\\le 2\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 6,\\quad 0\\le x + y\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x + y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 6,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\\\1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=1=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- u + v$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D x + y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_\\Delta v\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(v\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{2} [\\int_{0}^{6} v\\,\\mathrm{d}u]\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{2} v[\\int_{0}^{6} 1\\,\\mathrm{d}u]\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{2} v\\left[u\\right]_{0}^{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{2} v\\left(\\left(6\\right)-(\\left(0\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(6\\right)\\int_{0}^{2} v\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(6\\right)\\left[\\frac{v^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{2} \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(6\\right)\\left(\\left(2\\right)-(\\left(0\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(6\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right) \\\\ & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539320 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_D x + y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le x + y\\le 6\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le x + y\\le 6,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x + y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 6\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\\\1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=1=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- u + v$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D x + y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_\\Delta v\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(v\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{6} [\\int_{0}^{2} v\\,\\mathrm{d}u]\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{6} v[\\int_{0}^{2} 1\\,\\mathrm{d}u]\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{6} v\\left[u\\right]_{0}^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{6} v\\left(\\left(2\\right)-(\\left(0\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right)\\int_{0}^{6} v\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right)\\left[\\frac{v^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{6} \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right)\\left(\\left(18\\right)-(\\left(0\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(18\\right) \\\\ & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539333 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_D x + y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 4,\\quad 0\\le x + y\\le 2\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 4,\\quad 0\\le x + y\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x + y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 4,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\\\1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=1=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- u + v$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D x + y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_\\Delta v\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(v\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{2} [\\int_{0}^{4} v\\,\\mathrm{d}u]\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{2} v[\\int_{0}^{4} 1\\,\\mathrm{d}u]\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{2} v\\left[u\\right]_{0}^{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{2} v\\left(\\left(4\\right)-(\\left(0\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)\\int_{0}^{2} v\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)\\left[\\frac{v^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{2} \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)\\left(\\left(2\\right)-(\\left(0\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right) \\\\ & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539336 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_D x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y):2\\le \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{16 - y^{2}}}\\le 5,\\quad 2\\le y\\le 3\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n2\\le \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{16 - y^{2}}}\\le 5,\\quad 2\\le y\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{16 - y^{2}}}\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):2\\le u\\le 5,\\,2\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16 - y^{2}}} & \\frac{x y}{\\left(16 - y^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}\\\\0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16 - y^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=\\sqrt{16 - y^{2}}=\\sqrt{16 - v^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u \\sqrt{16 - v^{2}},\\ y=v$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_\\Delta u v^{2} \\sqrt{16 - v^{2}}\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(u v^{2} \\sqrt{16 - v^{2}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\sqrt{16 - v^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta u v^2(16-v^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\int_{2}^{5} \\left(u\\right)[\\int_{2}^{3} - v^{4} + 16 v^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =1\\int_{2}^{5} \\left(u\\right)\\left[- \\frac{v^{5}}{5} + \\frac{16 v^{3}}{3}\\right]_{2}^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =1\\int_{2}^{5} \\left(u\\right)\\left(\\left(- \\frac{243}{5}\\right)+\\left(144\\right)-(\\left(- \\frac{32}{5}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{128}{3}\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{887}{15}\\right)\\int_{2}^{5} u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{887}{15}\\right)\\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{2}\\right]_{2}^{5} \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{887}{15}\\right)\\left(\\left(\\frac{25}{2}\\right)-(\\left(2\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{21}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{887}{15}\\right) \\\\ & =\\frac{6209}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539352 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_D x + y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 7,\\quad 0\\le x + y\\le 5\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 7,\\quad 0\\le x + y\\le 5,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x + y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 7,\\,0\\le v\\le 5\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\\\1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=1=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- u + v$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D x + y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_\\Delta v\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(v\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{5} [\\int_{0}^{7} v\\,\\mathrm{d}u]\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{5} v[\\int_{0}^{7} 1\\,\\mathrm{d}u]\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{5} v\\left[u\\right]_{0}^{7}\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{5} v\\left(\\left(7\\right)-(\\left(0\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(7\\right)\\int_{0}^{5} v\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(7\\right)\\left[\\frac{v^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{5} \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(7\\right)\\left(\\left(\\frac{25}{2}\\right)-(\\left(0\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(7\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{25}{2}\\right) \\\\ & =\\frac{175}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539354 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_D e^{x^{2} + x + 3 y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y):1\\le x\\le 3,\\quad 1\\le x^{2} + 3 y\\le 3\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 3,\\quad 1\\le x^{2} + 3 y\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x^{2} + 3 y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\\\2 x & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- \\frac{u^{2}}{3} + \\frac{v}{3}$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D e^{x^{2} + x + 3 y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_D e^{x+(x^{2} + 3 y)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta e^{u + v}\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(e^{u + v}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} [\\int_{1}^{3} e^{u} e^{v}\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} \\left(e^{u}\\right)[\\int_{1}^{3} e^{v}\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} \\left(e^{u}\\right)\\left[e^{v}\\right]_{1}^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} \\left(e^{u}\\right)\\left(\\left(e^{3}\\right)-(\\left(e\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(e^{3} - e\\right)\\int_{1}^{3} e^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(e^{3} - e\\right)\\left[e^{u}\\right]_{1}^{3} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(e^{3} - e\\right)\\left(\\left(e^{3}\\right)-(\\left(e\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(e^{3} - e\\right)\\cdot \\left(e^{3} - e\\right) \\\\ & =\\frac{e^{2}}{3} - \\frac{2 e^{4}}{3} + \\frac{e^{6}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539366 :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}$ and $v=xy,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 4{x}^{2},\\,4\\le xy\\le 5\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,4\\le v\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}=-\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{4}^{5}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{v}^{2}}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{4}^{5}{v}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{4}^{5}{v}^{2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{1}^{4}]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{4}^{5}{v}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}{\\int }_{4}^{5}{v}^{2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}\\,{|}_{4}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{9}\\cdot (125-64) \\\\  & =\\frac{122\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539371 :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}$ and $v=xy,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 16{x}^{2},\\,2\\le xy\\le 5\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 16,\\,2\\le v\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}=-\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}{\\int }_{1}^{16}\\frac{{v}^{2}}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}{v}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{16}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}{v}^{2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{1}^{16}]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}{v}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡16-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}{v}^{2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}\\,{|}_{2}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{9}\\cdot (125-8) \\\\  & =26\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539379 :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}$ and $v=xy,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 9{x}^{2},\\,2\\le xy\\le 4\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 9,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}=-\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\frac{{v}^{2}}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{v}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{v}^{2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{1}^{9}]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{v}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{v}^{2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}\\,{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{9}\\cdot (64-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{112\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539380 :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}$ and $v=xy,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 16{x}^{2},\\,1\\le xy\\le 4\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 16,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}=-\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{16}\\frac{{v}^{2}}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{v}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{16}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{v}^{2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{1}^{16}]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{v}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡16-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{v}^{2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{9}\\cdot (64-1) \\\\  & =14\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539387 :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}$ and $v=xy,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 4{x}^{2},\\,5\\le xy\\le 6\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,5\\le v\\le 6\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}=-\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{v}^{2}}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{v}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{v}^{2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{1}^{4}]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{v}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{v}^{2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}\\,{|}_{5}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{9}\\cdot (216-125) \\\\  & =\\frac{182\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540183 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S(x-y)\\,\\text{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $r(u,v)=⟨2u+2v,\\,2u-2v,\\,3v⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 3$ and $0\\le v\\le 3.$\n\n*Hint: $‖r_u′\\times r_v′‖=2\\sqrt{34}.$*\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=2\\sqrt{34}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$f(r(u,v))=(2u+2v)-(2u-2v)=4v.$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S(x-y)\\,\\text{d}S&=\\iint_D f(r(u,v))‖r_u′\\times r_v′‖\\,\\text{d}A\\\\&=2\\sqrt{34}\\iint_D 4v\\,\\text{d}A\\\\&=2\\sqrt{34}\\int_0^{3}4v\\,\\text{d}v\\cdot\\int_0^{3}1\\,\\text{d}u\\\\&=2\\sqrt{34}[2v^{2}]_0^{3}[u]_0^{3}\\\\&=2\\sqrt{34}(18-0)(3-0)\\\\&=108\\sqrt{34}.\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540184 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S(x-y)\\,\\text{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $r(u,v)=⟨3u+v,\\,3u-v,\\,3v⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 2$ and $0\\le v\\le 3.$\n\n*Hint: $‖r_u′\\times r_v′‖=3\\sqrt{22}.$*\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=3\\sqrt{22}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$f(r(u,v))=(3u+v)-(3u-v)=2v.$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S(x-y)\\,\\text{d}S&=\\iint_D f(r(u,v))‖r_u′\\times r_v′‖\\,\\text{d}A\\\\&=3\\sqrt{22}\\iint_D 2v\\,\\text{d}A\\\\&=3\\sqrt{22}\\int_0^{3}2v\\,\\text{d}v\\cdot\\int_0^{2}1\\,\\text{d}u\\\\&=3\\sqrt{22}[v^{2}]_0^{3}[u]_0^{2}\\\\&=3\\sqrt{22}(9-0)(2-0)\\\\&=54\\sqrt{22}.\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540187 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S(x-y)\\,\\text{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $r(u,v)=⟨u+3v,\\,u-3v,\\,2v⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 2$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$\n\n*Hint: $‖r_u′\\times r_v′‖=2\\sqrt{11}.$*\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=2\\sqrt{11}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$f(r(u,v))=(u+3v)-(u-3v)=6v.$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S(x-y)\\,\\text{d}S&=\\iint_D f(r(u,v))‖r_u′\\times r_v′‖\\,\\text{d}A\\\\&=2\\sqrt{11}\\iint_D 6v\\,\\text{d}A\\\\&=2\\sqrt{11}\\int_0^{1}6v\\,\\text{d}v\\cdot\\int_0^{2}1\\,\\text{d}u\\\\&=2\\sqrt{11}[3v^{2}]_0^{1}[u]_0^{2}\\\\&=2\\sqrt{11}(3-0)(2-0)\\\\&=12\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540188 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S(x-y)\\,\\text{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $r(u,v)=⟨2u+2v,\\,2u-2v,\\,v⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 3$ and $0\\le v\\le 3.$\n\n*Hint: $‖r_u′\\times r_v′‖=6\\sqrt{2}.$*\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=6\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$f(r(u,v))=(2u+2v)-(2u-2v)=4v.$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S(x-y)\\,\\text{d}S&=\\iint_D f(r(u,v))‖r_u′\\times r_v′‖\\,\\text{d}A\\\\&=6\\sqrt{2}\\iint_D 4v\\,\\text{d}A\\\\&=6\\sqrt{2}\\int_0^{3}4v\\,\\text{d}v\\cdot\\int_0^{3}1\\,\\text{d}u\\\\&=6\\sqrt{2}[2v^{2}]_0^{3}[u]_0^{3}\\\\&=6\\sqrt{2}(18-0)(3-0)\\\\&=324\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540196 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S(x-y)\\,\\text{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $r(u,v)=⟨u+2v,\\,u-2v,\\,v⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 2.$\n\n*Hint: $‖r_u′\\times r_v′‖=3\\sqrt{2}.$*\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$f(r(u,v))=(u+2v)-(u-2v)=4v.$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S(x-y)\\,\\text{d}S&=\\iint_D f(r(u,v))‖r_u′\\times r_v′‖\\,\\text{d}A\\\\&=3\\sqrt{2}\\iint_D 4v\\,\\text{d}A\\\\&=3\\sqrt{2}\\int_0^{2}4v\\,\\text{d}v\\cdot\\int_0^{1}1\\,\\text{d}u\\\\&=3\\sqrt{2}[2v^{2}]_0^{2}[u]_0^{1}\\\\&=3\\sqrt{2}(8-0)(1-0)\\\\&=24\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540204 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x^{2} - x y - 3 y=5.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(-2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we set $f(x,y)=x^{2} - x y - 3 y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=5.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-2,-1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(-2,-1)=f_y(-2,-1)i-f_x(-2,-1)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x^{2} - x y - 3 y)\\,i-\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x^{2} - x y - 3 y)\\,j \\\\ &=(- x - 3)\\,i-(2 x - y)\\,j \\\\ t(-2,-1)&=(- \\left(-2\\right) - 3)\\,i-(2 \\left(-2\\right) - \\left(-1\\right))\\,j \\\\ &=(-1)\\,i-(-3)\\,j \\\\ &=-i+3\\, j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-i+3\\, j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540215 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $- x^{2} - x y - 3 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}=-4 - e.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,e).$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we set $f(x,y)=- x^{2} - x y - 3 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-4 - e.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,e)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,e)=f_y(1,e)i-f_x(1,e)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x^{2} - x y - 3 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,i-\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x^{2} - x y - 3 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,j \\\\ &=(- x - \\frac{3 x}{y})\\,i-(- 2 x - y - 3 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,j \\\\ t(1,e)&=(- \\left(1\\right) - \\frac{3 \\left(1\\right)}{\\left(e\\right)})\\,i-(- 2 \\left(1\\right) - \\left(e\\right) - 3 \\ln{\\left(\\left(e\\right) \\right)})\\,j \\\\ &=(- \\frac{3}{e} - 1)\\,i-(-5 - e)\\,j \\\\ &=-\\left(1 + \\frac{3}{e}\\right)\\, i+\\left(e + 5\\right)\\, j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-\\left(1 + \\frac{3}{e}\\right)\\, i+\\left(e + 5\\right)\\, j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540216 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $- 3 x^{2} + 4 x y + 4 y=0.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we set $f(x,y)=- 3 x^{2} + 4 x y + 4 y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(2,1)=f_y(2,1)i-f_x(2,1)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- 3 x^{2} + 4 x y + 4 y)\\,i-\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- 3 x^{2} + 4 x y + 4 y)\\,j \\\\ &=(4 x + 4)\\,i-(- 6 x + 4 y)\\,j \\\\ t(2,1)&=(4 \\left(2\\right) + 4)\\,i-(- 6 \\left(2\\right) + 4 \\left(1\\right))\\,j \\\\ &=(12)\\,i-(-8)\\,j \\\\ &=12\\, i+8\\, j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $12\\, i+8\\, j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540219 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $4 x^{2} + x y - y=17.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we set $f(x,y)=4 x^{2} + x y - y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=17.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(2,1)=f_y(2,1)i-f_x(2,1)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(4 x^{2} + x y - y)\\,i-\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(4 x^{2} + x y - y)\\,j \\\\ &=(x - 1)\\,i-(8 x + y)\\,j \\\\ t(2,1)&=(\\left(2\\right) - 1)\\,i-(8 \\left(2\\right) + \\left(1\\right))\\,j \\\\ &=(1)\\,i-(17)\\,j \\\\ &=i-17\\, j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $i-17\\, j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540230 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $- x^{2} + 4 x y - 4 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}=-5 + 4 e.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,e).$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we set $f(x,y)=- x^{2} + 4 x y - 4 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-5 + 4 e.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,e)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,e)=f_y(1,e)i-f_x(1,e)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x^{2} + 4 x y - 4 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,i-\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x^{2} + 4 x y - 4 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,j \\\\ &=(4 x - \\frac{4 x}{y})\\,i-(- 2 x + 4 y - 4 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,j \\\\ t(1,e)&=(4 \\left(1\\right) - \\frac{4 \\left(1\\right)}{\\left(e\\right)})\\,i-(- 2 \\left(1\\right) + 4 \\left(e\\right) - 4 \\ln{\\left(\\left(e\\right) \\right)})\\,j \\\\ &=(4 - \\frac{4}{e})\\,i-(-6 + 4 e)\\,j \\\\ &=\\left(4 - \\frac{4}{e}\\right)\\, i+\\left(6 - 4 e\\right)\\, j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $\\left(4 - \\frac{4}{e}\\right)\\, i+\\left(6 - 4 e\\right)\\, j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540232 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $- 3 x^{2} - x y + x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}=-14 + 2 e.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(-2,e).$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we set $f(x,y)=- 3 x^{2} - x y + x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-14 + 2 e.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-2,e)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(-2,e)=f_y(-2,e)i-f_x(-2,e)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-2,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- 3 x^{2} - x y + x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,i-\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- 3 x^{2} - x y + x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,j \\\\ &=(- x + \\frac{x}{y})\\,i-(- 6 x - y + \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,j \\\\ t(-2,e)&=(- \\left(-2\\right) + \\frac{\\left(-2\\right)}{\\left(e\\right)})\\,i-(- 6 \\left(-2\\right) - \\left(e\\right) + \\ln{\\left(\\left(e\\right) \\right)})\\,j \\\\ &=(2 - \\frac{2}{e})\\,i-(13 - e)\\,j \\\\ &=\\left(2 - \\frac{2}{e}\\right)\\, i-\\left(13 - e\\right)\\, j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $\\left(2 - \\frac{2}{e}\\right)\\, i-\\left(13 - e\\right)\\, j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540245 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $- x^{2} + x y + 4 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}=12 + 2 e^{2}.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(2,e^{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we set $f(x,y)=- x^{2} + x y + 4 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=12 + 2 e^{2}.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,e^{2})$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(2,e^{2})=f_y(2,e^{2})i-f_x(2,e^{2})j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(2,e^{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x^{2} + x y + 4 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,i-\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x^{2} + x y + 4 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,j \\\\ &=(x + \\frac{4 x}{y})\\,i-(- 2 x + y + 4 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,j \\\\ t(2,e^{2})&=(\\left(2\\right) + \\frac{4 \\left(2\\right)}{\\left(e^{2}\\right)})\\,i-(- 2 \\left(2\\right) + \\left(e^{2}\\right) + 4 \\ln{\\left(\\left(e^{2}\\right) \\right)})\\,j \\\\ &=(\\frac{8}{e^{2}} + 2)\\,i-(4 + e^{2})\\,j \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{8}{e^{2}} + 2\\right)\\, i-\\left(4 + e^{2}\\right)\\, j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $\\left(\\frac{8}{e^{2}} + 2\\right)\\, i-\\left(4 + e^{2}\\right)\\, j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540255 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $4 x^{2} + x y - 3 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}=7 - e.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(-1,e).$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we set $f(x,y)=4 x^{2} + x y - 3 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=7 - e.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,e)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(-1,e)=f_y(-1,e)i-f_x(-1,e)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(4 x^{2} + x y - 3 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,i-\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(4 x^{2} + x y - 3 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,j \\\\ &=(x - \\frac{3 x}{y})\\,i-(8 x + y - 3 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,j \\\\ t(-1,e)&=(\\left(-1\\right) - \\frac{3 \\left(-1\\right)}{\\left(e\\right)})\\,i-(8 \\left(-1\\right) + \\left(e\\right) - 3 \\ln{\\left(\\left(e\\right) \\right)})\\,j \\\\ &=(-1 + \\frac{3}{e})\\,i-(-11 + e)\\,j \\\\ &=-\\left(1 - \\frac{3}{e}\\right)\\, i+\\left(11 - e\\right)\\, j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-\\left(1 - \\frac{3}{e}\\right)\\, i+\\left(11 - e\\right)\\, j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540264 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $- 3 x^{2} + x y + 4 y=-6.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we set $f(x,y)=- 3 x^{2} + x y + 4 y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-6.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,-1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(-1,-1)=f_y(-1,-1)i-f_x(-1,-1)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- 3 x^{2} + x y + 4 y)\\,i-\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- 3 x^{2} + x y + 4 y)\\,j \\\\ &=(x + 4)\\,i-(- 6 x + y)\\,j \\\\ t(-1,-1)&=(\\left(-1\\right) + 4)\\,i-(- 6 \\left(-1\\right) + \\left(-1\\right))\\,j \\\\ &=(3)\\,i-(5)\\,j \\\\ &=3\\, i-5\\, j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $3\\, i-5\\, j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540266 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $- 3 x^{2} - x y - y=-1.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we set $f(x,y)=- 3 x^{2} - x y - y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-1.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,-1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,-1)=f_y(1,-1)i-f_x(1,-1)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- 3 x^{2} - x y - y)\\,i-\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- 3 x^{2} - x y - y)\\,j \\\\ &=(- x - 1)\\,i-(- 6 x - y)\\,j \\\\ t(1,-1)&=(- \\left(1\\right) - 1)\\,i-(- 6 \\left(1\\right) - \\left(-1\\right))\\,j \\\\ &=(-2)\\,i-(-5)\\,j \\\\ &=-2\\, i+5\\, j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-2\\, i+5\\, j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540271 :question/problem "![](images/e4/e46488d991bf461eacc51ecfcd490497bf13fcdf2b7ff998b54fdc501caf40ab.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=3\\,i$ and a square $C$ with side length $10$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/a2/a2964076c4126d6277625260c45479d1a4fb2028f7e75223a0f5942b15f5a2d2.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $F=3\\,i$ is parallel to ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{4},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net outflow through ${C}_{1}$ will cancel with the net inflow through ${C}_{3}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s\\,⟹\\,{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540277 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as $r(t)=7\\cos t\\,i+3\\sin t\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,2\\pi].$\n\n![](images/88/88907a8e0d33f889ae4fe3723a239b904a302f41fa294fc372e9d73cb049128a.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=0-(-1)=1.$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D)&=\\iint_D\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x.$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$r'(t)=\\langle -7\\sin t,3\\cos t\\rangle.$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x&=-\\int_0^{2\\pi}3\\sin t\\cdot(-7\\sin t)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=21\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\sin^2 t\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=21\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\frac{1-\\cos 2t}{2}\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(\\frac{21}{2}-\\frac{21}{2}\\cos 2t\\right)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\left[\\frac{21}{2}t-\\frac{21}{4}\\sin 2t\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\&=\\left(21 \\pi-0\\right)-\\left(0-0\\right)\\\\&=21 \\pi\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\text{Area}(D)=21 \\pi.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540286 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as $r(t)=5\\cos t\\,i+6\\sin t\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,2\\pi].$\n\n![](images/07/078f9509a08715c2a842bc9b8e12bfb3da9b9d77950275174ac684b399d06a68.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=0-(-1)=1.$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D)&=\\iint_D\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x.$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$r'(t)=\\langle -5\\sin t,6\\cos t\\rangle.$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x&=-\\int_0^{2\\pi}6\\sin t\\cdot(-5\\sin t)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=30\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\sin^2 t\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=30\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\frac{1-\\cos 2t}{2}\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(15-15\\cos 2t\\right)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\left[15t-\\frac{15}{2}\\sin 2t\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\&=\\left(30 \\pi-0\\right)-\\left(0-0\\right)\\\\&=30 \\pi\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\text{Area}(D)=30 \\pi.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540298 :question/problem "![](images/07/078757ceefd30bb15326569c8fdd2f58b92a1a1cf9679c0d1ea662f387cbe553.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C = C_{1}∪C_{2}$, where\n\n- $C_{1}$ is parametrized by $r_{1}(t) = 7t^{2}i - 3t^{3}j$ for $t ∈ [- 1, 1]$, and\n- $C_{2}$ is parametrized by $r_{2}(t) = 7i + 3tj$ for $t ∈ [- 1, 1]$.\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region." :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${r}_{1}(t)$ and ${r}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\, & {r}_{1}^{′}(t)=14t\\,i-9{t}^{2}\\,j \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\, & {r}_{2}^{′}(t)=3j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}y\\,dx-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-3{t}^{3})\\cdot 14t\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3t)\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =42{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t-0 \\\\  & =42[\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =42\\left(\\frac{1^{5}}{5}-\\frac{(-1)^{5}}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =42\\left(\\frac{1}{5}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =42\\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{84}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540301 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as $r(t)=2\\cos t\\,i+7\\sin t\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,2\\pi].$\n\n![](images/92/92ce9f04a78465f03756605514aab3c48396c66eb8863f16a64b563a8cace6ee.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=0-(-1)=1.$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D)&=\\iint_D\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x.$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$r'(t)=\\langle -2\\sin t,7\\cos t\\rangle.$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x&=-\\int_0^{2\\pi}7\\sin t\\cdot(-2\\sin t)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=14\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\sin^2 t\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=14\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\frac{1-\\cos 2t}{2}\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(7-7\\cos 2t\\right)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\left[7t-\\frac{7}{2}\\sin 2t\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\&=\\left(14 \\pi-0\\right)-\\left(0-0\\right)\\\\&=14 \\pi\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\text{Area}(D)=14 \\pi.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540302 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as $r(t)=6\\cos t\\,i+8\\sin t\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,2\\pi].$\n\n![](images/b3/b3fb73115ecc17ea35a3530602fe5851cba5a5a82c5bddb466d19fa82faf1d42.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=0-(-1)=1.$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D)&=\\iint_D\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x.$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$r'(t)=\\langle -6\\sin t,8\\cos t\\rangle.$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x&=-\\int_0^{2\\pi}8\\sin t\\cdot(-6\\sin t)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=48\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\sin^2 t\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=48\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\frac{1-\\cos 2t}{2}\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(24-24\\cos 2t\\right)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\left[24t-12\\sin 2t\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\&=\\left(48 \\pi-0\\right)-\\left(0-0\\right)\\\\&=48 \\pi\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\text{Area}(D)=48 \\pi.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540312 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as $r(t)=3\\cos t\\,i+6\\sin t\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,2\\pi].$\n\n![](images/d0/d08df891c30fe6d4777fbbfd86887741d224beb3d71af7d20001521a417115a4.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=0-(-1)=1.$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D)&=\\iint_D\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x.$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$r'(t)=\\langle -3\\sin t,6\\cos t\\rangle.$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x&=-\\int_0^{2\\pi}6\\sin t\\cdot(-3\\sin t)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=18\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\sin^2 t\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=18\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\frac{1-\\cos 2t}{2}\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(9-9\\cos 2t\\right)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\left[9t-\\frac{9}{2}\\sin 2t\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\&=\\left(18 \\pi-0\\right)-\\left(0-0\\right)\\\\&=18 \\pi\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\text{Area}(D)=18 \\pi.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540320 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3 t - 3,\\,y=t,\\,t\\le 0,\n$$\n\ndetermine the range of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ evaluate the endpoint: $x(0)=-3$. The coefficient of $t$ is $3$, so the coordinate is monotone on the given half-line. The parameter endpoint is included. The range is $x\\le -3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540324 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=- 2 t - 2,\\,y=t,\\,t\\le -3,\n$$\n\ndetermine the range of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ evaluate the endpoint: $x(-3)=4$. The coefficient of $t$ is $-2$, so the coordinate is monotone on the given half-line. The parameter endpoint is included. The range is $x\\ge 4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540326 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=- t^{2} - 2 t - 5,\\,y=t^{2} - 6 t + 5,\\,t\\le 3,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ complete the square:\n\n$$\nx=- t^{2} - 2 t - 5=- \\left(t + 1\\right)^{2} - 4 .\n$$\n\nThe vertex parameter is $t=-1$, which is admissible. The vertex value is $-4$ and is attained. The coordinate is unbounded below. Consequently, $x\\le -4.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ complete the square:\n\n$$\ny=t^{2} - 6 t + 5=\\left(t - 3\\right)^{2} - 4 .\n$$\n\nThe vertex parameter is $t=3$, which is admissible. The vertex value is $-4$ and is attained. The coordinate is unbounded above. Consequently, $y\\ge -4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540328 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=- t^{2} + 2 t + 2,\\,y=- t^{2} - 6 t,\\,t<3,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ complete the square:\n\n$$\nx=- t^{2} + 2 t + 2=3 - \\left(t - 1\\right)^{2} .\n$$\n\nThe vertex parameter is $t=1$, which is admissible. The vertex value is $3$ and is attained. The coordinate is unbounded below. Consequently, $x\\le 3.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ complete the square:\n\n$$\ny=- t^{2} - 6 t=9 - \\left(t + 3\\right)^{2} .\n$$\n\nThe vertex parameter is $t=-3$, which is admissible. The vertex value is $9$ and is attained. The coordinate is unbounded below. Consequently, $y\\le 9.$"}]}
